            <div xml:id="d472">

                <pb facs="0510" n="500" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg500"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/10515: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-12T22:00:00-04:00">April 12, 1944—10
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>651. Your 1234, April 11. We have given full consideration to your recommendation
                    that the threat clause be eliminated from the ball-bearing note but in view of
                    the importance which we attach to this <hi rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> and
                    the general attitude of interested agencies here (see Department’s 637, April
                    11) feel constrained to retain the clause in question particularly since London
                    informs us that though the British are not happy over retention of the clause
                    they have nevertheless instructed Mallet to associate himself fully with your
                        <hi rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> even should the Department and FEA
                    decide to retain the threat.</p>
                <p>Sent to Stockholm; repeated to London as Department’s No. 2897.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
